22:05:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Family stories can preserve real memories of difficult conditions and difficult people—especially from the Soviet era, where authoritarian institutions, violence, repression, and rigid gender expectations affected many lives. It makes sense that those stories would influence how you interpret similar experiences now. But a belief being passed down for generations can also become a lens that selectively reinforces itself: people notice examples that fit it, share those stories, and treat counterexamples as exceptions. That does not mean your grandmother, mother, or you were dishonest; it means inherited experience is not the same as a reliable judgment of every man from a very broad population. It is also worth avoiding collapsing Soviet history, Russia, and Ukraine into one fixed “type.” Ukrainians have had varied experiences across regions, generations, languages, families, and political outlooks—especially since Ukraine’s independence and the current war. You can take the practical lesson from your family’s experience without adopting the group judgment: - Assume the colleague may prefer direct, concrete communication—but ask rather than presume. - Keep interactions professional and set clear boundaries from the start. - Don’t interpret seriousness, reserve, or bluntness as hatred without specific conduct. - If he is insulting, intimidating, or repeatedly disrespectful, treat that as a workplace problem and address it directly. That is not denying the past. It is refusing to make a new colleague carry responsibility for it before he has done anything.
