22:06:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not have to “drop your guard.” The alternative to stereotyping is not blind trust; it is using **behavior-based caution**. You can protect yourself from being blindsided with any new colleague by: - Keep early interactions professional and work-focused. - Put important decisions, deadlines, and agreements in writing. - Clarify roles and escalation paths upfront. - Prefer meetings with agendas and follow-up notes for contentious topics. - Set a calm boundary the first time someone becomes disrespectful: “I’m happy to discuss the work, but not if we’re interrupting or raising voices.” - Document repeated incidents with dates, exact words/actions, witnesses, and work impact. - Involve your manager or HR promptly if there are threats, harassment, intimidation, discrimination, or a recurring hostile pattern. - Seek support from trusted coworkers rather than handling serious problems alone. Those measures reduce risk without requiring you to assume this colleague is dangerous or hostile because he is Ukrainian and male. In fact, they are more reliable than a nationality-based prediction: they respond to the actual warning signs that matter.
